## Why This Is Asked

Tests a realistic, hard-to-reproduce real-time collaboration scenario that involves concurrency, offline edits, presence, and conflict resolution.

## Key Concepts

- Real-time synchronization and OT/CRDT approaches
- Multi-context orchestration and test isolation
- Offline/online reconciliation and conflict resolution
- Network conditioning and latency measurement
- Per-document data seeding and cleanup

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright pseudo-skeleton for three users
async function testRealtimeCollab() {
  const ctx1 = await browser.newContext();
  const p1 = await ctx1.newPage();
  const ctx2 = await browser.newContext();
  const p2 = await ctx2.newPage();
  // ... navigate, simulate edits, verify presence
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale to 10+ concurrent collaborators?
- How would you validate final state after reconciliation with random edits?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests at the beginner level often miss realistic offline scenarios and deterministic retries. This question probes practical E2E thinking: isolation, network control, and idempotent submission.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end orchestration of multi-step flows
- Auto-save timing and state persistence (e.g., localStorage/IndexedDB)
- Offline/online transition handling and reconciling state
- Mocking external APIs (identity) for determinism
- Ensuring single, idempotent final submit

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright sketch (context-level offline, data isolation, mocked API)
const context = await browser.newContext({ storageState: 'test-user.json' });
const page = await context.newPage();
await context.setOffline(true);
await page.goto('https://finapp/onboarding');
// perform step interactions, wait for auto-save marker, then go online
await context.setOffline(false);
// verify single submit
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to mobile vs desktop layouts?
- How would you validate that auto-save writes to the correct storage (localStorage vs IndexedDB) across browsers?

## Why This Is Asked
Examination of asynchronous post-payment work and data consistency across services.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end orchestration with a payment gateway and an async rewards service
- Idempotent checkout and idempotency keys
- Eventual consistency via polling or webhook retries
- Service mocks to deterministically reproduce success/failure
- Data isolation per tenant and quota edge cases

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright route mocks example
await page.route('https://api.stripe.com/**', route => {
  route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ id: 'pay_123' }) });
});
await page.route('https://rewards.service/api/credit', route => {
  route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ credited: true }) });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test webhook delivery retries and backoff strategies?
- How would you detect flakiness from eventual consistency in CI?

## Why This Is Asked
This task tests end-to-end capabilities for complex, multi-tenant data flows, cross-service parity, and auditability under concurrency.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant data isolation
- Background reconciliation jobs
- Cross-service consistency checks
- Feature flag variability
- Concurrency and race conditions
- Audit logs and retention

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-test asserting per-tenant SOT parity
const tenants = seedTenants(100);
for (const t of tenants) {
  const hashA = hash(fetch(t, 'Contacts'));
  const hashB = hash(fetch(t, 'Activities'));
  const hashC = hash(fetch(t, 'Deals'));
  assert(hashA === hashB && hashB === hashC, `Pariy fail for ${t.id}`);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify parity without hashes?
- How would you detect and recover from reconciliation drift in production?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for autosave/drafts cover offline-first UX, idempotent writes, and cross-device conflicts, which are common pain points in real apps.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first data flows and write queuing
- Idempotent REST writes and ordering guarantees
- Test data isolation via seeds and reset hooks

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright skeleton (pseudo)
test('autosave offline queue and merge', async ({ page, request }) => {
  await page.goto('/editor');
  await page.fill('#note', 'Initial');
  await page.waitForTimeout(31000); // wait for autosave
  // simulate offline
  await page.route('POST', '/api/drafts', route => route.abort());
  await page.fill('#note', 'Offline edit');
  await page.setOfflineMode(true);
  await page.fill('#note', 'More offline edits');
  await page.setOfflineMode(false);
  // verify upload order and no duplicates
  // reload or inspect draft store
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for WebSocket-based drafting?
- How would you test long offline periods and partial merges?

